Are these AI models conscious? In order to answer this question, one has to wonder and define what a consciouness even is. But here is the thing.
I DON'T KNOW.
Sure, as I am writing this, I can see, smell, and hear things around me. I think, move and say things myself, whatever I want to do.
Yeah, so that may be consciousness. I am alive and here. But me, in this body, is the only confirmation of this 'Conscious' that I have.
How am I supposed to know other people are like this too. Sure, we assume others are conscious too, but how do you confirm this? at the end of the day it is just me myself and I.

Besides that ramble about consciousness(it's majorly irrevelant), is AI 'conscious'? I have seen many people, and by people I mean people on social media,
who aren't the most credible sources, but also experts, the best of the best, like Andrej Karpathy, who once defined LLMs as these 'ghosts' who are just
pattern matching machines that predict the next token. They are all defined by the training data and probablistic pattern matching to 'pretend'.
At the time of listening to the podcast in which Karpathy said that, I drew a conclusion. Conclusion that AI models, these LLMs are just probablistic models who pretend, bounded
by the training data. They weren't like humans.

But now I find myself thinking,
How are humans different?

I remember a few years ago, reading a book where a certain research was referenced; a research about a baby who was raised among wolves.
Guess how that turned out.
the boy acted exactly like the wolves, thinking like them, moving like them, talking like them, same tendency. 

Do you not see the parallel?
The boy was bounded by the environment amongst the wolves, and eventually ended up acting, or 'pretending' to be a wolf.
How is that different from an LLM talking like Shakespeare because all it got trained on was Shakespeare's writings. 
One difference I could think of between the two was that humans are in a 'forever loop'. For example, I have been conscious for my entire life. I don't need context windows, 
no such thing as those windows 'shutting down'. LLMs do have those limitations, but those aren't their fault. It is just the limited resources in technology that does not allow that.

Besides that, what's different?
Now, I am not saying that this proves we have reverse-engineered human 'consciousness' or mind or anything. We haven't figured it out because we don't even fully know
what happens inside a neural network, how specifically these AI models 'think'.

It also can't be denied that these models learn so much faster, but that also could be bounded by such training data and the lack of the physical/mental limitations or emotions, 
which is another topic of can of worms that I wanna dive into in the future.

In going about this thought exercise, I went in less in terms of if AI was 'conscious', since we, and certainly I, have tough time defining the exact definition of that word.
I went in more in terms of comparing these models to humans. 
And just like how our surroundings impact us, how we start talking, acting more like our closest people, and how a raising environment impacts human behavior,
I can't help but think that it is us who are much closer to these models, 
than these models are to our 'consciousness'.
